<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oregon Country</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oregon Country was the region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> west of the Rocky Mountains between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and Russian America. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> agreed in 1818 to occupy it jointly, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> dominated its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. American settlement along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a> in the 1840s led to the Oregon Treaty of 1846, which divided it at the 49th parallel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oregon-country.jpg" alt="Oregon Country" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacobolus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1837%E2%80%931845).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>As the Treaty of 1818 defined it, the Oregon Country ran from 42° N to 54°40′ N and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> east to the Continental Divide. Oregon Country was the American name; the British called it the Columbia District. Throughout the period it was home to many independent Indigenous peoples, who received little recognition from either government. Native traders were vital to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, the region's main business until mid-century, and Chinook Jargon served as the common language between Indigenous groups and the small European population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> all claimed the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which took up Spain's claim in the Adams-Onís Treaty of 1819, rested its own on Robert Gray's entry into the Columbia River in 1792 and the Lewis and Clark Expedition; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on David Thompson's journey down the whole Columbia and earlier coastal exploration. Russia withdrew to 54°40′ N by treaties of 1824 and 1825. John Jacob Astor's Fort Astoria, completed in 1811, was the first permanent Euro-American settlement; captured by the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, it became Fort George. From 1824 John McLoughlin ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s operations from Fort Vancouver, whose company deliberately over-hunted the frontiers to leave American trappers a fur desert.</p><p>The Methodist Jason Lee built a mission school in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willamette-valley">Willamette Valley</a> in 1834, and from the early 1840s settlers came over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a>; the Great Migration of 1843 brought an estimated 700 to 1,000. That year settlers set up the Provisional Government of Oregon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champoeg">Champoeg</a>. The expansionist slogan Fifty-Four Forty or Fight! raised the stakes, but neither power wanted a third war in 70 years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty">Oregon Treaty</a> of 1846 left Vancouver Island British. The American part became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> in 1848 and Washington Territory was split from it in 1853.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former Oregon Country lies within the Canadian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, the whole of the states of Oregon, Washington and Idaho, and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and Wyoming. Partition, settlement and the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> brought a catastrophic fall in the Indigenous population after 1846, and their land was seized over the following two decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oregon Country” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under an 1818 agreement, Britain and the United States jointly occupied the <b>Oregon Country</b>, but neither governed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-oregon">Provisional Government of Oregon</a></li></ul></section>
